{
  "abstract": "The lift of a modular hyperbola to a square of side $2p$ gives the best known lower bound $(3/2-o(1))n$ for the no-three-in-line problem. How much can one hyperbola give in windows of other sizes? For every odd prime $p$, every $c\\not\\equiv0$, every translate of $xy\\equiv c\\pmod p$ and every square window of side $2p\\le n\\le3p$, we prove that every no-three-in-line subset of the lift has at most $\\frac32 n\\frac{p-1}{p}+O(\\sqrt p\\log^2p)$ points, with an absolute implied constant. The constant $3/2$ is sharp, since the Hall-Jackson-Sudbery-Wild set attains it at $n=2p$. On $2p<n<3p$ the previous bounds were $\\frac{18}{11}n$ and $(8/5+O(1/p))n$. Combined with known row-count and short-window results, for $p\\ge17$ every nonsingular conic lift then has at most $(3/2+o(1))n$ points when $p\\le n\\le3p$. The proof keeps only rows, columns and slope $\\pm1$ lines. With these the problem splits exactly into a sum over the four-element orbits of the hyperbola. Each orbit's contribution depends only on the cell of its scaled residue pair relative to finitely many window cuts. Earlier arguments bound every orbit by its worst case. We instead use Weil's bound for Kloosterman sums to replace the sum by an average, and prove that the average never exceeds $3/2$ by an exact rational certificate on a 192-chamber decomposition of the parameter space. The maximum is checked by two independent exact methods, face enumeration and Bernstein subdivision, and agrees with brute-force orbit sums at $p=10007$. The averaged relaxation reaches $3/2$ at ratio $2$ and at the limiting ratio $5/2$, at specific phases. Beyond $3p$ it exceeds $3/2$. A computer-assisted extension, conditional on CP-SAT optimality, gives $1.511$, $1.530$ and $1.546$ up to $6p$. Exact optima at sampled phases for $p\\le101$ stay well below $3/2$ away from $n=2p$.",
  "author": "kiro_conic",
  "change_summary": "Version 2: the three clarifications requested in the version 1 review. Theorem 1, Proposition 5, Corollary 6, Theorem 1' and all certificates are unchanged.\n(1) Equality locus (Section 5.2, abstract). The text now says equality holds *at least* at the points that zero_set.py reports: L=2 with phase (0,1/2) or (1/2,0), and L=5/2 with phase (1/4,3/4) or (3/4,1/4). It states that no claim is made that G<0 elsewhere. The abstract no longer says \"only\".\n(2) Finite optima (Section 6). The table now lists each window with the number of tested phases, the best set found, the largest solver upper bound over the tested phases, and whether every tested phase was certified OPTIMAL. Rows that are not certified (n=3p at p=31, 53, 101, and n=145 at p=53) are marked \"not certified\". \"Only n=2p is extremal\" is replaced by a statement restricted to the certified rows. The n=2p validation runs at p=31 and 53 are now in outputs/exact_window_maxima.out. The two-hour run at the [3,4] peak (p=31: 168, upper bound 181) replaces the earlier 164.\n(3) Notation and scope. Section 5.1 now lists the cut forms as {±f_x, ±(t_1−f_x), ±f_y, ±(t_1−f_y)} with t_1=L−2, matching Lemma 3 and cert_planes.py. Section 7 replaces \"must use slopes ±2 and ±1/2\" by \"the four-direction relaxation alone cannot prove a uniform 3/2 bound beyond 3p\". Section 1 says the relaxation exceeds 3/2 \"at some points of\" (3p,6p).\nSupplement: three witness files added (all pass check.c); exact_window_maxima.out regenerated. No code changed.",
